# Build order for the core system. One package per line, blank lines and
# comments ignored. ./build.sh packages walks this from the top.
#
# Order is dependency order, not preference: musl has to exist before
# anything can link against it.

# glibc leads because udev is part of systemd and systemd does not build
# against musl. The two C libraries coexist: separate loaders, separate
# names, and every binary names the one it wants.
glibc

musl

# the shell. Static against musl, so it is the one binary in the image that
# does not depend on the loader or on any library being staged correctly --
# which matters because pinit execs plogin and plogin execs this.
bash

# The tools chapters 6 and 7 installed and chapter 8 replaces.
#
# The image had all of these already: m4, make, patch and file cross-compiled
# by the chapter 5 toolchain, bison, perl, gettext and texinfo built inside
# the chroot as temporary tools. They work, which is why nothing noticed they
# were still the temporary ones -- the book rebuilds every one of them in
# chapter 8 and this did not.
#
# The difference is not cosmetic in every case. Chapter 7's perl was built
# without threads, without man pages and with no idea where a pager is,
# because a perl that only runs other packages' configure scripts needs none
# of that. Chapter 7's gettext was three programs copied into /usr/bin with
# no libraries at all.
#
# m4 first, and before autoconf below, which is m4 at run time.
m4
make
patch
file

# autotools, before coreutils rather than anywhere more logical.
#
# coreutils applies a patch that touches tests/local.mk, which leaves
# Makefile.in older than the Makefile.am it is generated from, so make runs
# automake to bring it up to date and automake runs autoconf. On the build
# host that always worked, because the host had both. In the chroot there is
# no host, and coreutils stopped with "Error 127" -- command not found --
# four packages into the build.
#
# Neither compiles anything; they are perl and shell. What they need at run
# time is m4 and perl, which chapters 6 and 7 put in the chroot and which the
# build host has anyway.
autoconf
automake

coreutils

# the text tools every shell script reaches for. musl, like coreutils:
# these are standalone programs, nothing links against them.
sed
grep
gawk
findutils
diffutils

# the archiver and the compressor it reaches for first. Same story: standalone
# musl programs. Until these were here the image could not unpack a tarball,
# so it could not install anything into itself.
gzip
tar

# The compression libraries kmod reads modules with. Without all three,
# kmod -- and modprobe, depmod and insmod, which are symlinks to it -- cannot
# start at all: they name libz, liblzma and libzstd as hard dependencies
# whether or not a given module is compressed.
zlib
xz
zstd

# bzip2 is not one of kmod's three; it is here because tar cannot unpack a
# .tar.bz2 without it, and mtdev, elfutils, pcre2 and dejavu-fonts all ship
# one. The build host had bzip2, so this only surfaced in the chroot.
bzip2

# What util-linux links against, before util-linux rather than after it.
#
# These used to sit below, on the reasoning that they were "libraries
# util-linux was already linked against but that nothing staged" -- which was
# true, and was the symptom rather than the arrangement. util-linux was
# finding the *build machine's* ncurses and readline, linking them, and being
# followed by a staging step that put matching libraries in the image so the
# programs could start. Nothing in that chain built util-linux against this
# image.
#
# The sysroot ended it, and not gracefully: headers resolve inside obj now,
# so configure still found the host's libreadline -- AC_CHECK_LIB does not
# honour --sysroot -- enabled readline support on the strength of it, and
# then fdisk.c stopped on a readline/readline.h that is not there. A missing
# header is the good outcome; the same check passing against the host's
# library is how the old order stayed invisible.
#
# ncurses first: readline links libncursesw rather than a separate termcap.
# libxcrypt is here for sulogin.
ncurses
readline
libxcrypt

# The build tools and the toolchain, which the build host used to supply and
# the image therefore never had.
#
# Here rather than earlier because bc links readline, and readline is two
# lines up. Everything else in this tier could sit anywhere after glibc.
#
# pkgconf first, and it is the one that matters most: packages/common.sh
# aims PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR at the image, and nothing from libffi onward -- the
# entire Wayland stack -- configures without a pkg-config to ask.
pkgconf
bc
flex

# gperf generates the lookup tables udev matches its rules with. systemd's
# meson.build makes it a hard requirement and stops at configure time
# without it.
gperf

# binutils before gcc, because gcc's configure probes whatever assembler and
# linker it can find and enables its own features on the strength of the
# answers. gmp, mpfr and mpc before gcc for the ordinary reason: it links
# them. Unlike chapters 5 and 6, where those three are unpacked inside the
# gcc tree, these are real packages and gcc finds them installed.
#
# This tier is what makes the image able to rebuild itself. Until it existed
# the image could run what this workstation compiled and nothing else, and
# packages/gcc-runtime.sh had to copy libstdc++ off the host because building
# it meant building GCC.
binutils
gmp
mpfr
mpc
gcc

# after gcc, because libtool records the compiler and linker it was
# configured against
libtool

# bison after m4, which it calls at run time. The rest wait for perl, which
# waits for zlib and bzip2 -- it is built BUILD_ZLIB=False so it links the
# image's copies rather than the ones bundled in its own tree.
#
# gettext and texinfo are both perl at run time: autopoint is a perl script
# and so is makeinfo.
bison
perl
gettext
texinfo

# udev's dependencies, then udev. All of these are glibc programs: their
# libraries are linked into udev, so they have to agree on the C library.
#
# udevadm needs libblkid, libmount, libacl, libcap and libcrypto. attr is
# here only because acl needs it.
util-linux
attr
acl
libcap
openssl

# python, and the build systems written in it.
#
# meson is the reason this tier exists and it is not optional: kmod on the
# next line is configured with it, and so is nearly everything after --
# wayland, wayland-protocols, libdrm, pixman, libinput, libdisplay-info,
# libxkbcommon, mesa, wlroots, sway. Until now the build host's meson ran
# them all and nobody noticed the image had none, which is the same story as
# pkgconf and automake.
#
# expat first, and it moved up from beside dbus to get here: python is built
# --with-system-expat, and would otherwise use the copy bundled in its own
# tarball. Nothing else cared where expat sat.
#
# Then python -- the one with pip, unlike chapter 7's --without-ensurepip
# copy -- and the four modules pip needs as build backends before it will
# install anything with --no-build-isolation.
#
# ninja before meson, and bootstrapped by python rather than built by meson.
# That is what keeps the two from being circular: ninja needs a C++ compiler
# and a python, meson needs ninja to build anything at all.
expat
python
flit-core
packaging
wheel
setuptools
ninja
meson

kmod

# markupsafe and jinja2 before udev, and for udev alone. systemd generates
# several of its sources from templates at build time, and its meson.build
# checks for the module by importing it: "python3 is missing modules:
# jinja2" is where the build stopped.
markupsafe
jinja2

udev

# the editor and the pager. Both after ncurses, which they link for terminal
# handling.
vim
less

# the timezone database. Data only: zic compiles it, nothing links it.
tzdata

# the ext4 tools. After util-linux, whose libblkid and libuuid these link
# against instead of building their own.
e2fsprogs

# and the FAT tools, for the EFI system partition. From BLFS, not LFS.
dosfstools

# process monitoring. After ncurses, which top and watch link against.
procps-ng

# the message bus. Not an LFS package: the book builds no D-Bus, it only
# creates the messagebus user. iwd will not start without a system bus, and
# seatd and sway want one. expat, which it reads its configuration with, has
# moved up to the python tier -- python is built --with-system-expat.
dbus

# still to come, roughly in this order:
#
#   less         gzip's zless calls it, and man pages have nothing to page
#                with either
#   psmisc       fuser and killall; pidof already came with procps-ng
#
# iproute2 is no longer on this list as a requirement. Nothing in the boot
# path runs ip: pinit sets loopback with SIOCSIFADDR and iwd configures the
# wireless interface over rtnetlink. Still worth having interactively.

# wireless. init_os starts it through set_ip; it associates and applies the
# address itself over rtnetlink. Bundles ell, so there is no separate package.
iwd

# The Wayland stack, tier 1. Everything from here down is built against
# glibc, not musl: mesa and llvm are not realistically musl-buildable here
# and a stack cannot be split between two C libraries.
#
# libffi first -- wayland's protocol dispatch calls through it. wayland is
# the client and server libraries plus wayland-scanner, which is a build
# tool this machine runs. wayland-protocols is XML only. seatd hands out the
# DRM and input devices, which is what lets sway run without being root.
libffi
wayland
wayland-protocols
seatd

# Tier 2. The device and input layer.
#
# libdrm is the userspace side of the kernel's DRM; only the amdgpu driver is
# built, for the same reason DRM_I915 came out of the kernel config. pixman
# is the software rasteriser wlroots falls back to, which is what will make
# sway work in the VM. hwdata before libdisplay-info, which reads pnp.ids
# from it to name a monitor's manufacturer. xkeyboard-config is the layout
# data -- "us" and "latam" are files in it -- and libxkbcommon reads it.
# libevdev wraps the kernel input protocol for libinput in tier 3.
libdrm
pixman
hwdata
libdisplay-info
xkeyboard-config
libxkbcommon
libevdev

# Tier 3. Input handling proper.
#
# mtdev first: libinput hands it any device speaking the kernel's older
# multitouch protocol. Nothing here does, but the rescue USB may meet one.
mtdev
libinput

# Tier 4 prerequisites.
#
# gcc-runtime used to be here: libstdc++, libgcc_s and libgomp copied out of
# the host toolchain, "because building them means building GCC". GCC is
# built now, four tiers up, and installs all three itself -- so the copy had
# nothing left to do and said so plainly when it ran in the chroot:
#
#   install: '/usr/lib/libgcc_s.so.1' and '/usr/lib/libgcc_s.so.1'
#            are the same file
#
# gcc -print-file-name pointed at the destination, because the destination is
# now where the real library lives. Retired rather than guarded.
#
# mesa is the first C++ package here and enables OpenMP unconditionally, which
# is what libgomp was for. elfutils is here only for libelf, which mesa's
# meson.build hard-requires for radeonsi.
#
# cmake and glslang are mesa's too, and only for its Vulkan driver:
# meson.build makes glslangValidator a hard requirement whenever a Vulkan
# driver is enabled, glslang builds with nothing but cmake, and cmake
# bootstraps itself so it can go first. Both were supplied by the build host
# until mesa was configured somewhere the host does not exist.
cmake
glslang

# The two python modules mesa generates its own source with, and refuses to
# configure without: mako builds the GL dispatch tables and driver entry
# points, PyYAML reads the driver and pixel format descriptions. markupsafe,
# which mako needs, is already built for jinja2 further up.
mako
pyyaml
elfutils

# libglvnd before mesa: mesa has to know at configure time whether it is the
# dispatch layer or a vendor behind one.
libglvnd

# Tier 4. mesa: OpenGL, EGL, GBM and Vulkan.
#
# radeonsi for the Navi 23, RADV for its Vulkan, and no LLVM -- radeonsi
# switches to ACO without it, and llvmpipe, the only thing that truly needs
# LLVM, would only ever have been the VM's software renderer, which wlroots
# already has in pixman.
mesa

# Tier 5. Text.
#
# Nothing before this line can draw a letter. wlroots needs none of it; sway
# needs all of it, because its title bars and swaybar are cairo surfaces with
# pango laying out the text.
#
# freetype appears twice, under two names. It and harfbuzz depend on each
# other -- harfbuzz reads fonts through freetype, freetype's autohinter asks
# harfbuzz which glyphs are related -- so freetype is built once without,
# harfbuzz against that, and freetype again with. The second pass needs its
# own script because obj/.packages is keyed on the package name.
#
# glib comes before harfbuzz, not after: harfbuzz is built with -Dglib=enabled
# because pango passes GLib types across the harfbuzz boundary, so harfbuzz
# needs glib to exist first. glib itself needs only pcre2 and libffi.
libpng
freetype
fontconfig
fribidi
pcre2
glib
harfbuzz
freetype-harfbuzz
cairo
pango

# Tier 6. The compositor.
#
# json-c is sway's IPC format. wlroots is the compositor library and sway is
# the window manager on top of it; their versions are pinned to each other,
# sway 1.11 asking for wlroots-0.19. dejavu-fonts last, because fontconfig,
# pango and cairo can all be installed and still draw nothing without a font
# file to draw from.
json-c
wlroots
sway
dejavu-fonts

# Tier 7. A terminal and a launcher.
#
# tllist is a header fcft and foot both include, fcft is the font library
# foot renders with. sway's default configuration binds $mod+Return to foot
# and $mod+d to wmenu-run, so with these four the compositor stops being a
# wallpaper.
tllist
fcft
foot
wmenu
